INT: And what is your number?

MS: The number is A-4257.

INT: Tell me about getting the tattoo and what that meant to you.

MS: Well, in-- in a way, it was a relief-- that we figured, if we are being tattooed, then-- then they-- probably, they're going to keep us alive for a while. Otherwise, why would they bother tattooing us? That was a-- I considered it as an identification.

And we were told that we're going to be taken to another camp. But we didn't know-- we didn't know when. And a couple of days-- a couple of days later, we're there-- there was another transport that came over from Birkenau. And in that crowd was my father's partner.

And he's-- he was actually older than my father. And he asked me-- we were looking through the window, and they were in the yard, lined up. And he asked me where my father was. And I told him that he went to the left. And he started crying. And that was the last I saw of him also.

And I guess he got taken to another camp. I was in Auschwitz for three weeks.

INT: Do you know who made the selection with your father, with--

MS: I-- I think it was Dr. Mengele, but I-- I couldn't be sure. I was told that it was Dr. Mengele who made the selection when-- when we arrived. And

INT: And how long were you in Auschwitz, then?

MS: Three weeks.

INT: And what did your days consist of, in-- in Auschwitz?

MS: Mostly, they took us down, and they gave us military training and we were mutzen up, mutzen down. And that's-- that the little hat. Take it off and put it back on, and-- left, right, left, and we were just marching around. And we were even taught to sing some German songs. And then they would just-- let us back to go into the room.

INT: Do you remember any of the songs?

MS: No. And, most of the time, we were not-- not allowed to go down, except when we were-- we were drilled. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] And we were there-- we kept dreaming about back home, because we were still fresh. And then a holiday came. And--

INT: What holiday?

MS: It's the holiday that-- Pentecost. It's seven weeks after Passover. And, back home, during that holiday, my mother used to bake cakes, and-- and-- and we had two different meals-- a daily meal, and then, an hour later, we had a mid meals.

And it was a very joyous holiday. The house used to be decorated with flowers and all that. And all we could do is talk about what was-- what that holiday, what it meant back home.

And, after three weeks, we were loaded up on-- on trucks, and we were taken to the other camp.

INT: Which camp was that?

MS: That was Gleiwitz. As it turned out, it was called Gleiwitz Eins-- Gleiwitz I. Supposedly there were four camps in-- in Gleiwitz.

And after we were assigned to barracks, the next day we were lined up, and we were taken to a factory-- which turned out to be a huge railroad repair-- repair shop. And that's where we were assigned to different sections. I was assigned to-- my-- called [NON-ENGLISH] 20. And I did-- the job was to clean the rust off the tenders-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the-- the outside, and the inside, clean the water tank from the sand and get the rust out in on the tops and get the rust out. And the-- then it was painted.

And then I was transferred to another section. And then I was brought to dig ditches outside-- outside the factory. And it was a-- once or twice, we could hear the planes fly-- fly by. During the day, the American planes were flying over-- overhead.

And then I was the-- the-- the supervisor, the German civilians-- supervisor of the group, he asked me for-- asked them to put me back in his-- in his group. And so I was put back in that group, to work. And there was a young man, probably one year younger than me, and his name was Mendel.

And we-- we got so-- so close together that everybody took us for brothers. And that helped both of us, because you know, we were not singled out. We were not alone. And we tried to help each other. He did more to help me than I helped him, but it-- it worked out [INAUDIBLE].

Like when it came to [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they handed the winter coats, that I first received a-- a striped-- just a regular, you know, jacket that the camp was given. And he-- he took it. He grabbed it from my hand, and he went back, and he grabbed me a-- a regular winter coat that had, in the back, cut out-- a piece of the cloth was-- was cut out, and that-- the striped piece was put in it, so they'll know that we are from the camp.

And h-- he-- he helped me, and-- and-- and-- and I helped him. And we-- we became like brothers, in all this time that we were in the camp. Everybody took us for brothers.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] then he got to work in the kitchen, and I thought, well, I have somebody who's working in the kitchen. I'll-- I won't be hungry. But, the next day, when I came to work, and the German supervisor asked me where Mendel was, and I told him, oh, he's working in the kitchen, and he said, no, no, he's going to-- he has to come back to work over here.

And so he requested him. A day later, he was-- he was back working over there with me. And he-- he was very disappointed that he had to come to work in the factory. So he-- he requested that-- to be transferred, because he was a blacksmith. And he told them, I want to work as a blacksmith.

So they transferred him to a different section. That's when I-- and he also got to stay in another-- another block. And that's where I lost my other brother.

INT: Tell me about your-- the living conditions at the barracks. Were they better than Auschwitz? Were they as crowded?

MS: Yeah, because each one of us had a-- had a bunk, by himself. They were three-- three-storey bunks, and each one of us had a-- they were a little narrower. They were not-- not more maybe than 20 inches wide. But-- and we had a blanket. And the bunks were OK.

And I tried to avoid beatings, not to be hurt. But then, one night, somebody in that section, he didn't want it-- it was snow, outside, and cold, and he didn't want to go to-- he was afraid to go to the-- the bathroom. But he had to go, so he did it in a-- in a dish. And he spilled it out through the window.

And this SS officer happened to walk by. So, the next day, the-- the commander of the-- the block-- he was a-- he was a German, but he was a communist. And so he was in the-- he was also in the camp, but he was the-- the camp-- the block commander. And he wanted to find out who did it.

And so he took the three of us that we were sleeping in that-- that area, and he-- he started beating us. And he hit me over the head a couple of times, and I didn't-- I didn't let out a-- sound. I didn't make a sound.

But another guy, he started crying. And the more he was crying, wilder he got-- more-- more he beat him. He was just-- he became just like an animal. And it took a long time.

I don't know how the man survived that beating. I got away with just a-- he had a rubber hose in his hand that he used to use to beat us. And I-- I got three-- three hits over my head. And, since I didn't make a sound, he-- he stopped.

INT: And did the man admit to--

MS: No, I-- I had an idea who has done it, but I wouldn't-- I wouldn't give him away. Because, then, that would have been the end of him. So we all kept-- I-- I kept quiet about it. And the other one was just screaming, and he hit him so much that he couldn't even talk anyhow. So--

INT: And how were you able to take care of him, afterwards, for medical treatment-- or were you? Or--

MS: The-- we-- there was a-- a-- after, we had to go see a doctor. There was a-- a doctor from my hometown. And then there was another doctor-- who also we were-- who was also like-- like we were-- haftling.

And if for any reason we went to the infirmary, but he tried not to, because we-- we figured if we stay sick or something like that, they-- they're not going to let you stay in-- stay in the-- stay alive. But at one time, I burned my hand. But the skin came off my-- my hand. And I had to go to the infirmary. And it was bandaged with some paper bandage. And I-- I got to stay two days not to go to work.

And that was-- that was about all. And three days-- the third day, I had to go back to work with my-- my fingers were all raw, but--

And while I was in the camp, again, the-- we could see the American planes fly over. And the-- the SS were hiding. They had special bunkers for one-- one person-- little concrete bunker. And you could hear them talk to each other in this.

And when the planes flew by, they dropped some-- some kind of a flare. And they would say, oh, we hear them. We hit-- they-- they were saying to each other that the flak has hit one of the planes. But they didn't do it. It was just something that the planes had dropped.INT: Yes, do you have a question?

MS: No.

INT: Would you please continue with the story?

MS: Yeah. Well, in-- in the factory, we were-- we were under civilian control. And-- and, at one time, I was working at a different section, and there was German civilians. And we were talking about what-- what is happening. And-- and the man said, well, that they only bought you because we need-- we need workers.

So I said, if they wanted us, just the workers, why didn't they just bring us young people to work? Why did they have to bring the families and-- and exterminate them? And he said, no, that's not true. And he denied it. And so we-- we-- we brought up that we didn't want to continue with-- with the the conversation.

And in the-- in the factory, the SS were not inside-- the guards-- only-- only a sergeant, who came in with-- when they-- they brought us lunch in from the camp, when the lunch was distributed. And--

But the-- the guards, they-- sometimes they wanted to have-- there was a few who were quite decent. We had-- we were not too much in contact with them, but some of them were really mean. And if he saw one of us in the factory, sometimes he called them over, and he says, come over here. And when you came close to an SS, you had to take your cap off.

So there was several occasions where he took the cap away from him, and he threw it out the door. And he said, go get it. And the man went to get it. And, once he was on the other side of the gate, he shot him. And he-- and he said he tried to escape, and he shot him.

So the-- we-- we heard about it. And we tried to-- to avoid to-- even to go close to a gate so we wouldn't run in--

But the-- the sergeant was-- he wasn't too-- he wasn't cruel or-- or-- he was just a-- a overseer. And one time, he-- we were sitting at noon. There was tables and benches set up, and the-- the plates were put out-- 10 plates to each side. And, at one time, I was-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I was hungry. And as I went and I grabbed one plate, I took it with me, and I sat down, and I-- and I hid it under the table. With my knee, I pressed it against the table.

And so, they all started looking. And the-- the-- the SS came, and they-- they were looking for the-- they said I-- this I did. I counted 10, and there's only 9. And when this was going on, I was already sorry that I took it. But I couldn't just put it back up. I was holding it, with my knee, up against the table.

And they looked under, but I-- I-- I guess they didn't-- didn't see. And finally they brought another plate for the-- for the 10th person. And after we were finished with the first plate, I took the plate and-- and shared it with-- with my friend.

And there was occasions where we were hungry enough to-- to go and steal . And, at-- at one time, there was-- we were sitting after we finished dinner, and I was the first one sitting by the-- there was a little fence, where we would sit and have lunch.

And the sergeant found a-- a plate of-- of food that it was served. And he was going around, from one end to the other, and everybody was just holding their hands out to-- to get that extra plate of food. And he went back and forth about twice. And he-- finally he came-- I didn't-- didn't have my hand out.

He came over to me and he handed me the plate of food. And everybody was just looking-- why did he pick me up? I don't know why, but-- maybe because I didn't have my hand-- hand out. And, again, I shared the food with the-- well-- and--

INT: What kind of food would you be served at Gleiwitz?

MS: Oh, some kind of a soup. Maybe it had the little potatoes in it, some kind of a-- maybe some oats or-- or-- oat bran-- soup and potatoes and just-- we all looked if it has more potatoes, because that was a little heavier.

But it was never enough. In the morning, we got a-- a quarter of a bread and some kind of a coffee. And-- and the-- the soup was some-- I didn't know what was in it-- some potatoes and some [INAUDIBLE] there.

And then, after the-- we went back, we got another piece of bread or another soup, in-- in the camp. I would say that it-- it had about 800, 900 calories a day-- something like that.

INT: What did you do to keep your spirits up? Were you able to keep your spirits up?

MS: There was things that-- well, it-- it-- you just-- things happened to you without-- without planning them. Like we were given-- getting-- every two weeks, we were getting 2 marks-- concentration-camp money. And there was a cantina in the camp, where we were able to buy a soup or a-- a pack of cigarettes.

And, although I didn't smoke, I-- I bought the cigarettes. And first I traded the-- the-- the cigarettes with-- for bread. And then I thought that the person who's giving me his bread is-- I'm taking his life away, because he's not eating. And so I didn't-- didn't want to trade for bread.

But, in-- in the factory, we also had Russians working in the camp who were not-- who were like civilians. They were-- they were free-- they lived in a camp, but they were free to come and go in-- into the city.

And so we sold-- I sold the cigarettes for-- for 4 marks. That was regular German money. And-- and, for that, we-- we bought some Cokes. Because the water was very bad, very alkaline. And at--

INT: I'm sorry-- you bought Coke?

MS: Well, like a Coke--

INT: Coca-Cola?

MS: --no, not Coca-Cola-- some-- some kind of a

INT: Soft drink?

MS: --like a club soda, or had a little-- little flavoring in it--

INT: Uh-huh?

MS: And a-- and it was-- so it was-- there was a civilian who had a concession, a German civilian. And he was not allowed-- he wasn't supposed to sell us or anything. We were not supposed to have regular German money. But, instead of 5 pfennigs, we used to pay 10 pfennigs-- we used to put the money down. And then, when nobody saw, he took a bottle out and-- and put it down for us.

And that saved us, because the water was very alkaline. And once you start-- if you drank the water, you-- you got the runs, and that was it. You couldn't-- couldn't stop it. It was nothing you could do. So by avoiding to drink the water, that-- that helped us. And so we were always trading the cigarettes for-- to have that German money, this-- the regular money, to buy the soda.

And then, on one occasion, we went into a tender, to-- to clean it, and we found a lot of purses-- leather purses that were dumped. Somebody had put them in there, and they were-- they tore the lining out. They were looking if there was any hidden things there, in them. And they left them there.

And so we took the-- the purses, and we sold them to this Russian. And, again, we got some marks. The-- those purses were all from people that were taken from Theresienstadt. Because there was evidence that-- it says 'Theresienstadt.' And-- but we don't know where they-- when they were put in there, were--

But they came in handy, because we were able to buy bread for it, and that-- every day we sold a purse. And for the money, that Russian got us bread even. We [INAUDIBLE]. And so we had--

One day, we had-- we had just bought two loaves of bread, and we kept them in the toolbox. And, as we were inside the-- the-- somebody got on up there and-- and they stole the bread away. We came-- we came out of--

At around 9 o'clock, we had our 15-minute coffee break, or a 10-minute. And so we came out to eat. [LAUGHS] And it was-- it was empty. Somebody must have noticed it, that we had that-- we had that bread.

So we-- we were always trying to-- to steal, or-- or, if you were lucky, you got away with it.

INT: And the concentration-camp money, you were paid for how much work? How long were you paid?

MS: Every-- first, we were-- we got 2-- 2 mark. And then, later on, this-- where we-- where I got-- if we were considered as a better worker, we got more. At the-- at the end, I was-- I was getting 16-- 16 mark, every two weeks.

But then the-- they changed the camp commander. And that camp commander had closed the canteen. So we were-- we couldn't use it. I had accumulated already, I think, about 130-- 30 mark, and there was no way of even spending it. Because it was only good in-- in the camp.

But then, at-- at coffee break, we used to sneak in and try to say the prayer and-- and put the-- the-- the prayer things on on our arm and-- and head. We used to put them on, make-- say-- make a prayer, take it off, and give it to the next one.

And we had one-- one prayer book. There was also a-- a-- who was a rabbi, with us. And each day, we were-- we had our-- we're going to be over here or over here. We didn't know which wagon. And so we used to sneak in and-- and-- and-- and pray. And when we came home, we used to get together behind one barrack, certain people that wanted to pray. And we used to participate and say the prayers.

And, one day, they-- the-- our prayer book got confiscated. Somebody found it. So we-- we all give all those marks that we had. We-- we gave-- I don't know-- it cost us a couple of hundred marks, to get back the prayer book, because we only had one prayer book, between all of us, that we shared. And we-- we couldn't spend that camp money anymore.

INT: After being in Birkenau and Auschwitz and--

MS: Gleiwitz.

INT: --and Gleiwitz, and you prayed every day, do you--

MS: I tried to, yeah.

INT: Did you think someone was listening to your prayers?

MS: Well, I was-- this-- I'm glad you mentioned it. It was-- I figured there was some miracles happened, because we only-- we only had one-- one spoon and a-- and a metal [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] dish, you know, for-- for the soup. And at one time, I took a piece of welding-- welding rod-- because they-- they used it to weld the-- weld on all the-- the-- the wheels that were worn down, they used to weld on them and then cut them down, put them on a lathe, and-- and build up the steel on those wheels. And they-- for that, they used a quarter-inch welding rod.

And one time, I-- I took a piece of welding rod, and I-- I bent one side, for a handle. And the other side, I hammered out. And-- and I made, like, a-- like a knife. And when I-- and I went to a certain area where there was a grinding wheel, and I went to sharpen it.

And I was caught by one of the big shots from the-- from the factory, a German civilian. He was civilian, but he-- I-- I-- he was one of the, I think, running that place. And he-- he caught me sharpening that thing.

And he came and there, and he said, what are you doing? And so the-- I didn't any-- you know, to lie this. I told him I'm making a knife.

And he said, why are you making a knife? And I said, well, to cut the bread. And he said, don't you have a knife to cut the bread? I said, no, we don't have-- we don't have knives. And he just walked away and didn't do anything. Because he-- he could have had me shot for making a knife. We were not supposed to have knives. The knife, I just used in the-- in the factory, and I left it there. But I--

And-- because we wanted to slice the bread thin, to make it last longer. And that was a-- that was a lucky thing, because he could have reported me. That was-- would have been the end.

And there are many times that I could-- I could see miracles that-- that happened to me, that--

INT: Can you tell me about another miracle that-- that happened to you, that you can think of?

MS: As one-- one time, I-- you know, a miracle, when I stole that dish of food and they didn't see it. Because I could have been in deep trouble for doing that. And, one time, this friend of mine went to-- after work, we went to-- to peel potatoes. And we got a dish of food, for that. And he tried to steal some-- to bring home-- bring back to the barrack-- bring me some raw potatoes and some beets. And he got caught, and he-- he got really beaten up.

So, the next day, I said, I'm going to go. And we had just gotten some-- the pants that were-- riding pants, from Italian officers-- or Italian army pants-- who were wide on top but they were tight in the-- on the leg. They were pants made for boots.

And I was given those-- and, on top of those pants, I had the pants that I got at work. And-- because we were doing special-- special work where-- where dampness and all that. So we were-- we got extra jacket and pants.

And I-- and I had those pants and-- and jacket on, from-- from the factory. And I stole some potatoes and carrots and all that, and I put them in my-- in my pants. And, when it-- it came to the end of-- before we were leaving, we were all searched.

And I was searched, and he was going down over here. And I didn't have nothing. But if he would have gone a little higher, I had all kinds of potatoes and all that. That would have-- I kept it a little higher.

And he didn't catch me. I came-- came back to the barracks, and I told him, this friend of mine, well, here's for you.

INT: What would have happened to you, had you been caught?

MS: I would have gotten a terrible beating.

INT: How many people were in your barracks, at this time?

MS: There was probably 150 of us in that barrack.

INT: And they-- they weren't all Hungarians though.

MS: At first, we were all Hungarian Jews. And then we were-- then we were changed, because there was-- they-- they-- they changed in the factory, and they brought in more-- some more Polish Jews. And so we got-- we got a little mixed. And we were--

We were transferred over, because they made a-- a night shift. And so, some barracks were-- they made where they-- people were working nights, were sleeping in another barrack. And those that were working days were sleeping in-- staying in another barrack. And that was another-- another thing that, one day, one of the-- the--

When the people came in, that-- to work for the night shift, one of them thought he's going to be smart, and he mixed himself in with the day shift. And then, when they counted it-- counted us, they showed-- they came up with one too many. So they counted the night shift, and they came up with one too short.

So they had us line up according to the [NON-ENGLISH], the section as we were working, until they-- they found the person who-- he thought he's going to go back to the camp without spend the night at work. And they-- they-- they took him out, and they put him between--

We were always lined up, five with each other, and we all had to cross arms, holding arms-- lock arms, when we were walking home. And they put him between me and my-- and my friend.

And I told my friend, I-- I can't hold on to him, because I know this-- this is the end of him. I knew they're going to do something to him. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I let go. And then the SS-- the guards came, and he hit me with a rifle butt. And he said, you hold on to him.

And so I held onto him, and then I let go again . And I said to my friend, I don't-- my friend said they-- they going to kill you, if you don't hold on. And I said, I don't care. I don't-- I know they're going to finish him, and I don't want to hold on to him.

So, again, I let go of him. And again, the SS came and hit me with a rifle. Then he held on to me. And when we-- when we arrived in the camp, as soon as we got to the gate, he was away from-- from me. And the next day, when we came, he was displayed. He was dead and--

They said he was trying to escape, and he was shot. And he was displayed, so we could-- all can see him over there. And I knew that that's what's going to happen to him. And I didn't want to hold on to him.

And this has happened several times. And I said, if he escapes, that's his thing. I-- I don't want to hold on to him. And-- and he held on to me.

And, in another case-- it was just before the end of war-- some guy sat down, and he fell asleep. And again, they counted us, and they went to-- were short one. And they-- and they grouped us up according to-- and till they find the man. He was asleep.

And, on the way, they put him at the end, this time. And there was a young sergeant-- was very-- very mean. And-- and he shot him and-- and he said-- and he to he's-- he said he's just trying to escape so he can get couple of days off. That was the reward.

INT: The Germans were rewarded for--

MS: For-- yeah. Yeah.

INT: --catching--

MS: If they were-- Yeah. If it was that he-- somebody tried to escape, and he--

INT: Were the planes still flying overhead? Could you still hear them--

MS: Yeah, at first, we were-- we were taken down when there was a air siren. And we were taken down to a basement. And I was worried about it. I-- I felt very uncomfortable in that basement, because I figured that basement could be flooded and finish us off in no time. So that--

Only a couple of times we were taken down to the-- to the basement. After that, when there was a-- planes were flying by, those air raids, they just left us as-- left us in the factory. They-- they had their bunkers-- little-- for one-- one person, where they went into. And--

But we were left. We were just under the-- under the wagons, in-- in the factory and luckily, the factory wasn't bombed. Although we were hoping it would be. But it was never bombed.

INT: While you were unguarded, were-- would you ever think of escape? Or would-- did people--

MS: There was-- there was no--

INT: --make any plans--

MS: --there was no-- there was no-- and only one-- another thing, here, I was lucky. When we first got to-- to Gleiwitz, the barrack across from our barrack was not finished. And when-- after work-- and I went to look around, what's in the other barrack, and I saw a bunch of--

At that time, there was-- there was-- we were still mixed. There was some Polish and-- and Russian haftlings in that-- in-- in the camp. But I didn't know who-- we all had different-- different markings on the-- the number on the jacket. And I walked in the door, and I looked, and I saw they're all stopped over there. There was a bunch of dirt, in there, and they all-- they all stopped.

And, lucky again, I didn't walk in, because I don't know what they would have done with me had I walked in. Because, two days later, they escaped-- 15 of them-- or 16. I remember they were all Russians and Poles. And so then I thought about it. If I would have walked in, they would have had to-- to kill me or do something, because they would have been afraid that-- that I-- I expose them.

And, after that, they lined us-- lined us up, and they took all the-- the Poles and Russian out of the camp-- that camp-- and they-- they only left-- the camp was only with Jews in that camp. And we figured there is no escape, because it was-- all you do, you couldn't speak Polish. And if you didn't speaks Polish, definitely you had no chance.

And anyhow, later on, they-- they caught two of them that were escaped. But the-- the majority of them, they never caught.

INT: How do you know they caught two of them?

MS: Because they brought them back to the camp. And they-- and when we came back from-- from work, we were all-- all lined up in a square, and-- with the SS behind us, with the machine guns. And we were all forced to watch them being hung. And-- the two that they caught.

And, and it's-- several occasions, I was just-- plain, dumb luck, and we had that. Then they-- they fixed those barracks. And then another occasion, there was a-- they-- they changed-- every time somebody escaped, they changed camp commanders. And, after this escape, we got a very bad-- very bad commander, one who was very-- the rumor was that he had about a million people on his conscience.

And that's-- he's the one who closed the canteen and made it hard as possible. When we came back in the wintertime, and some of them only had that-- that pajama-like clothing and-- and that coat. So they put paper-- paper bags on-- on their back, to keep them warm. And they-- we-- he lined us up in-- in the square, and he had his German-shepherd dog. And those that were caught with paper on them, he made them roll in the snow, and the dogs were jumping on them and biting them. And, luckily, I-- I had a factory--

There, beside the camp clothes, I had the factory clothes and the coat. I wasn't-- didn't have any paper. And--

And then there was a-- an electrician in the factory. And as the-- he was working in the-- in the shop. And when the German civilian-- I guess he got a-- took a nap. And while he took a nap, this electrician took-- put on his clothes with his pass, and he walked out. And he was never caught-- I hope. And-- because he was never brought back.

And, after-- luckily they-- they changed the commander, after he escaped. Because every-- after every escape, they-- they changed commanders. And he got out.

INT: As the war came-- excuse me. As the war came to a close, did you notice a difference in the treatment of the soldiers, toward you--

MS: No--

INT: --or the S--

MS: --no, there was no difference. I-- the-- the-- the-- the civilian-- the-- the-- the-- who was in charge of-- of us, when I had-- when my birthday was, he came and he brought me a little green pepper for my birthday.

INT: A green pepper?

MS: Yeah. [LAUGHS] A little bell pepper. For my birthday. It was good. It was some vitamins. But--

And at one time, they-- they-- there was also a-- a kitchen, over there, a cafeteria for the German civilians. And they brought in loads of carrots and potatoes and-- and some of the people that were helping, that were working in my group, they stole some carrots and potatoes. And they came running.

And so I opened up the-- the toolbox. And they threw it in-- in the toolbox. And then I got on top of the toolbox. And I was working over there, using the toolbox as a stepping stool. And--INT: Would you please continue with your story?

MS: Yeah. And I stood on-- and then the-- the-- the German kapos-- the kapos came and-- and talked to me. And they said, where's your-- where's your workers because the-- the German-- I-- since I was the oldest-- longest worker in that section, he made me the-- the-- vorarbeiter, the foreman. And he said, where is your men? And I said, well, they're working in-- in the thing there. They were running to hide.

And then the-- there was a-- a Jewish kapo who was-- didn't behave himself. Well, he-- he came to me, and he said, [INAUDIBLE]. He gave me a big slap. I thought my head is going to roll off.

And-- and I says, my men is over here. They're working. And-- but they were not there. They were-- they run in the-- they-- the-- the other kapo said, they-- we saw them running over there. I said, no, they're working. They're in the-- in the-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] they're in the tender. They're cleaning.

INT: By 'tender,' is that-- is that the engine that you're referring to?

MS: No, that's the-- the back part of the engine. That carries the-- the water and the coal supply for the--

INT: OK.

MS: --for the thing. And worker-- and-- and so, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] we escaped. We-- we got away with it. If he would have told me to get off and-- and look in there, it-- it was full of potatoes and carrots that the-- that the guys stole. And--

INT: How did the end of the war come-- come, for you?

MS: Well, we were-- [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] we went to work and-- on a-- on a Thursday, I think. It was January 16 or-- I don't remember the exact date. And we-- we went to work in the morning. And all of a sudden, the-- the siren in the-- the factory sounded, and we were told to line up.

And we lined up, and we were taken back to-- to the camp. And that's when we were told that the camp is being evacuated. We knew that the Russians are getting closer and-- and closer.

And the next-- they gave us, each one of us, a loaf of bread. And, the next morning, they-- we were lined up, and we were marched-- we were marched out from the camp. And we were walking all day, till late at night.

And, again, I-- I had some shoes with some wooden-- wooden soles. And it kept slipping on the-- on the-- on the snow. And a few times, I-- I slipped, and I fell on to the-- to the SS man. And he-- he warned me, the first time. And-- and then it happened again, and he hit me with the butt. And he said, the next time it happens again, I'll just shoot you.

And as we were walking, we-- we could hear shots being fired. Those that-- that couldn't walk, that fell down, they just-- they just-- just shot him on the-- on the side of the road. And we walked till-- till late at night. And, at night, we-- we arrived to some area. And we were all locked in to a-- to a big warehouse room.

And the next morning, when they-- they opened up the doors, they let us out, we found ourselves behind a POW camp. It was Americans and English. And they told us that the Russians are only 3-- 10 kilometers behind-- behind us. And they broke through, and they-- they-- they told us to keep on-- hold on to-- you know, that we're going to be liberated soon.

And, that day, we walked again. They-- they walked us again, all till-- till late at night. And then they arrived in-- into a camp-- which, I found out it was called 'Blechhammer.'

And they put us in a barrack. And the next morning, they kept bringing in people from all different camps. So I got-- went out in the camp to look if I could find my brother or somebody who knows about him and-- or somebody from my hometown.

And, as I was walking and looking around, I found myself a-- with-- with some German officers, and they selected-- made a selection. They-- they watched me walk. And, since I-- I had a hard time walking, because of the wooden soles, I kept slipping. My-- my legs got very swollen. And I had trouble walking, so they put me on the side. And they took the others out.

And then I went back to the-- that barrack where I was staying. And all of a sudden, all the Germans, all the-- the-- the SS left. So I went outside the camp, and I went into the SS barracks. And I found sugar, and I filled my pockets up with sugar. And five loaves of bread I-- I brought back.

And when I got back to the barrack, I gave-- because we were just five of us in the group that we knew each other. And so gave each one a loaf of bread. And-- well, I also took a quilt from the SS with me, because all we had is a-- just a-- a blanket.

And then, all of a sudden, the next day, the SS came back. And I was afraid they're going to come in and-- but they-- they lined us up outside, and they-- they counted. And then they told us to go back in the barrack. And-- and they left.

In the meantime, they-- they didn't leave completely. They-- they were outside, and they-- they used to get up on the-- on their [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] observation posts. And anybody they could see, they-- they were just machine-gunning them down. Those that were outside. I tried not to go outside.

Then we ran out of the bread. And so I went to where they kept the food. And there was-- there was nothing left but some flour on the floor. So I took some flour together from the-- from the floor.

And, in the meantime, the-- the water-- we had no more water. The water stopped running. So, there was some of-- it was a shooting going on, and some of the barracks got-- got shot-- got bombed. And so I took some wood from the barracks, made a fire, and I melted the snow and took the flour and made some-- made some bread, like a matzahs. [LAUGHS] And I took a bottle and rolled it out and-- and-- like tortillas-- and-- and baked it.

And then we were there, and the-- some of the friends said, let's leave. Let's leave. And I said, no, it's-- it's not time yet.

In the meantime, we were-- the camp wound up being right in the middle of the-- of the battleground. And some of the cannons shot through the-- the camp was surrounded maybe a-- with a 10-foot-high wall. And some of the cannons shot through the-- through the wall. There was a hole in-- holes in the wall.

And some of them got out through there. And I said, no, it's-- it's not time yet. And I kept holding off until, finally, I saw the SS came back. This was around Wednesday night. And they surrounded one of the barracks. And they-- they burned the barrack down. And they-- anybody who tried to escape, they-- they shot.

And, the next morning, I-- I- I went and I saw that there was just-- you-- you couldn't even recognize that it was a person or a log, except you could see the-- where the glasses were on-- on his-- on his head.

INT: How many people were in those-- that barracks that they burned?

MS: I don't know, because they-- they kept bringing from all the surrounding areas they-- they-- they brought them, in those camps not far that they called 'Buna,' where they made that artificial rubber, and the coal mines over there.

And so, that Thursday, I looked around, and there was-- the-- the campground was just full of dead people. And the next-- the next night, Thursday night, there was a big battle going on. We could hear the Katyushas-- the-- the rockets flying, and the-- the bomb-- big-- the cannons. And we could hear there was a big battle going on.

And the barrack right across got hit by a shell, and it was just demolished. And some of them that were in their bunks were just laying there. It killed them. And so, this friend of mine came to me, and he said, well, what shall we do? And I said, I think it's-- this-- I think it's time to go. Because I figured that, if we stay in the camp, there is a chance we-- we could get sick-- we could get typhus. Because of-- nobody took care of the dead people. They were just laying all over.

And so I said, well, I think it's time to go. So we got out of the-- and, as we got out through the wall, we could see in-- we were in-- like, in a forest. It was just hundreds and hundreds of dead haftlings from-- that were in the camp that were-- they were shot. Because they used to come in and take some people out and-- and shoot them in the-- in the forest.

And, as we kept walking-- and finally, there was a gate. And we opened up-- there-- there was a door there, and we opened up the door. And then we heard a motorcycle. So we didn't know if it's Germans or-- or who it was. And so we closed the gate and we-- behind the fence.

And we-- we peeked through, and we saw that it was a Russian on the motorcycle. So we-- we opened up the gate, and he came through. We could see that the battle was-- had passed-- that there was-- there was a battleground going on there, but it was past.

And then we saw a-- a German tank man who was just-- half his body, burning-- half-- half was gone. It just has the body burning. And so we knew that it--

So we started walking in the opposite directions. And then we were approached by some Russian soldiers who asked us for cigarettes. [LAUGHS] And we could-- so there was some of us who could speak with them-- speak Russian. And they told them that we didn't have any cigarettes or bread.

INT: So the Russians were your liberators? Or--

MS: Yeah. And then we came into a town, where the German civilians had-- had abandoned. And we got into a house, and we found clothes over there. And I changed the shoes-- changed the clothes and put on another coat. And-- and there-- there was food. And I told the rest of them, let's stay over here a little bit, and-- where we can get our strength back, and--

But the others, they were-- they-- they said no, because-- just in case the Germans put on a-- a-- an offensive and they-- and they come back, then we are being caught again. And they-- they said, no, no, let's go further back.

And as we started-- we got out of that house, and we started walking. We could see the town burning. And we could see there was a big battle in the night before. And-- and we were stopped by a Russian officer again, who was conducting traffic. And he says, go further back. Don't stop for another 12 kilometers.

So we kept walking back until we-- till we reached a house. And we knocked at the door, and the people over there, they-- they said they were Poles. But they put us up. They made us dinner, and they put us up for the night, and they made us breakfast. And the next morning, we started walking, because we wanted to get back home.

INT: Did you ever get back home?

MS: Yeah, it took me four weeks to get back home.

INT: What did you find there?

MS: At-- well, at first, we were-- we were going-- we thought we're going to take the train. We were going to go through a certain area. And then, when-- when we got to the Polish-Russian border that the Russians had occupied, they took us off the train, and they locked us up. And we told them we came-- they said, no, you cannot go across the border. If you want to go, go back over there and sign up with-- with a transport.

And so we went back to the city and we took the train. They put us on the train back. It was-- I don't know how far we had traveled. And we-- as we went to sign up with a transport, all of a sudden a Czechoslovakian soldier comes, and he starts talking to this friend of mine. And it wound up they're from-- they were from the same city, and they-- they knew each other.

And so he told him-- told him, don't go with this transport, because this is the transport is-- you're going to wind up in Russia. And most of these are going to be questioned, how-- how come they wound up in Germany during the war? They're going to be, first, accused as being traitors. And he said, don't-- don't go. You're going to just wind up in a Russian camp.

And so we-- he said, come back and I'll-- I'll-- I'll put you up. So there was the-- the three of us. And he-- he put us up in a-- in a room, and he brought us food from the army. And he--

Then he said, well, there's one way you can go. Tell-- go and tell them you want to join the Czechoslovakian army. And there is a transport that it's going to go through Poland to Slovakia. And tell them you want to sign up over there.

And so we did, and we told them we want to sign up with the Czech army. And so we waited there for about two weeks, until there was a transport from Poland through Slovakia. And we drove through the mountains and--

And when we got to Slovakia, there was already some Jews that had returned. And so they-- they put us up. And, slowly, we started to walk and--

INT: Uh-huh?

MS: So it took me four weeks to get back to my hometown.

INT: And how did it come to be that you came to the United States?

MS: Well, my grandparents were here. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I wanted to get in touch with them. And somehow I managed to get in touch with them, because they send me-- send me money. And the communists didn't-- didn't like that. And, since I was also a-- a Zionist, that--

I had a friend of mine who was in the police, and he warned me that I have-- I should leave the-- leave the town. Otherwise-- otherwise I will be-- I'll be arrested, because they have a-- a dossier on me.

INT: A dossier? Is that--

MS: Yeah--

INT: Uh-huh.

MS: --a file.

INT: Right.

MS: They have a file on me. And they--

INT: And when did you finally come to the United States, then?

MS: And so, we-- the friend of mine came back to the American zone. And so he told me to-- to go with him. And-- and we started out, going with him.

And then we-- we were going to cross the border from Romania to Hungary. And as we were over there, we met some man, and he said, where are you from, and I told him. And he says--

He had a paper, and he said I'm-- I'm supposed to look you up to reorganize this Zionist organization. So I told him I-- I had to leave because I was warned that way. So he gave me a-- an address to-- to go to, in Budapest. So, when I got to Budapest-- [DRINKS]

There was the three of us. And then-- in [INAUDIBLE] finally I wound up having 17 people under my supervision. And so we waited for a transport to cross over to-- to Austria. Finally, about two weeks later, we were able to cross to Austria.

When I got there, I was made supervisor of the whole room. And I had 56 people to hand out the food, distribute the food, and all that. Then, a-- a week later, we were-- we were loaded on-- on a train to come-- to cross over to the American zone. But, for some reason, they-- our train didn't-- was not allowed to go-- to cross the border. So, we were-- we had to go back to Vienna.

By the time we went back to Vienna, there was already another transport in the room. So we were doubled. Instead of-- I had 120 people. And it meant I was-- I had a hard time, because I only got the same amount of food to distribute. But somehow, we-- we managed.

And-- and a week later, we were put on a train again. We were crossed to Austria, to the American zone. And, when we got there, again I was-- I was looked up. And--

INT: Yeah.

MS: --I was asked to stay, to help out. And so they-- they just opened that refugee camp, and they need help over there. So I stayed there.

INT: And then, when-- when did you finally come to this country?

MS: Well, I-- I came to this country in January '49. In the meantime, I got in touch with my grandparents. And, when I left-- when I left Romania, I had to leave-- my sisters didn't even know where I-- that I left and where I went. Then I tried to send them a message. And-- but I guess they didn't get the message.

Then this friend of mine went back. And I asked him to look my sisters up. And he-- somehow, he let them know that I was in Austria. So, it took a while before they-- they followed me and they came over.

INT: What did you do for a living, when you came to this country?

MS: I worked as electrician, and first I-- [LAUGHS] I started working for-- I was making $0.40 an hour.

INT: Did you have a family to support? Did you--

MS: I-- yeah, I had--

INT: --did you have children?

MS: --one-- one child--

INT: Yeah.

MS: --when I came. My daughter was one year old when we came to this country. But I was staying with my grandparents. So--

INT: And how many children do you have now?

MS: Four.

INT: OK.

MS: One was born in New York, and two of them were born in Los Angeles.

INT: OK. You know, can we take a look at some of your pictures, right now? Yeah?

OK, you want to tell me about this picture?

MS: Yeah, this is a-- a picture of us. That-- that was taken in 1938. There's only seven of us. My mother was pregnant with-- with the eighth child.

INT: Can you identify the people in there for us?

MS: Yeah. There's my mother, my father, me and my brother--

INT: Wait. Tell me their names, as well.

MS: There is me, the-- the tallest. [LAUGHS]

INT: Yes? And you are?

MS: I was 13, at the time. And my brother Hugo was 12. And there's my sister, to the right-- Rose. Then is my sister Adele. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] No, that was-- was-- it's Zipora.

And my father and my sister Adele and my little brother Tobias. And there's my mother and my sister Anne.

INT: And what is your mother's name?

MS: My mother's name was Blanka.

INT: And your father's name?

MS: Leib.

INT: OK,

MS: And the two-- the two on the end are-- are alive.

INT: And who-- who's still alive?

MS: Anne, and, to the right, Rose. She lives in Florida. Anne lives in Detroit. And there is me, on the--

INT: OK?

MS: That's a-- a premium mark. It's equivalent to a German mark. It was concentration camp money that we received as a premium for working in the factory.

INT: OK.

MS: That's me, Morris Samuel Sobo. I was sitting by the desk in the camp office. That was in Enns. The camp was called 'Green Shelter.' I was the office manager, at the time.

INT: And this was what kind of camp?

MS: A-- a DP camp--

INT: OK--

MS: --after the war. That picture was probably taken in 1947.

INT: OK?

MS: This is a picture of my grandparents, Joseph Sobel and Jenny Sobel. This picture was taken in Brooklyn, probably in 1950.

INT: OK.

MS: That's a picture of my four children. Blance, who was the oldest. She was born in Austria. And Lee. He was born in New York.

And in the white shirt is my son Hugo. And in the blue shirt is my son Simon. They were born in Los Angeles.

INT: And where was this picture taken?

MS: This was probably [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] early '60s or '60--

INT: And was-- where was it taken?

MS: In Los Angeles.

INT: OK.

MS: This is a picture of my daughter Blance and my granddaughter Debbie. The picture was taken in-- probably last year-- 1954.

INT: I'm sorry-- what year?

MS: 1954.

